  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$",
